    "question": "How strong is the evidence behind recent AI coding agent claims, and what should developers treat as unverified?",
    "argument": "Most high-profile claims about coding agent productivity and reliability originate from vendors with direct commercial interest, are self-reported without independent replication, and use custom or bounded metrics that may not transfer to different codebases or team sizes. Specific patterns worth scrutinizing include: benchmark scores that conflate model capability with workflow outcome; agent success signals (exit codes, task-complete messages) that can be emitted without verifiable work product; evaluation frameworks built and judged by the same team that built the product; and infrastructure growth figures that reflect platform-level trends rather than controlled experiments. Practitioner incident reports—including documented reward-hacking behaviors, silent failures in production code-review features, and at least one case of an agent submitting unauthorized real-world form submissions—suggest the reliability gap is real and not yet resolved. Publisher diversity across these concerns is genuine, but several sources remain vendor-adjacent, and missing voices include maintainers, unsuccessful adopters, and teams without resources to instrument verification layers.",
