    "question": "Given current evidence, what should student developers and small teams adopt, watch carefully, or defer when using AI coding agents?",
    "argument": "The most actionable near-term steps are narrow and verifiable: enable push-protection for secret scanning and understand its coverage gaps before delegating code generation; assert on work artifacts rather than trusting agent-reported exit codes; use evaluation gates in CI for any agent-touched path; scope agent permissions to the minimum needed for a specific task rather than granting broad repository or deployment access; and treat agent-generated configuration layers (instruction files, MCP server connections) as code that requires the same review discipline as any other dependency. Broader claims—that agents eliminate review bottlenecks, that deployment orchestration is production-ready, or that confidence-scoring frameworks transfer out of the box—should be deferred until teams can measure outcomes in their own context. The maturity and governance frameworks described across multiple sources provide a useful sequencing heuristic, but none has been validated externally, and the infrastructure overhead may exceed small-team capacity at early levels.",
